
naive_bayes_classifier.py   Main Python classifier script
fake_or_real_news.csv       Dataset (6,335 labeled news articles)
pipeline_results.csv        Output: metrics table (auto-generated)
figures/                    Output: all 6 figures (auto-generated)
README.txt                  

-----------
REQUIREMENTS
-----------
Python 3.9 or higher (developed and tested on Python 3.11)
No obscure packages — all are available via pip.

Required libraries:
  pandas
  numpy
  matplotlib
  seaborn
  scikit-learn
  nltk

-----------
INSTALLATION
-----------
Install all dependencies with one command:

    pip install pandas numpy matplotlib seaborn scikit-learn nltk

On some Linux systems you may need:

    pip install pandas numpy matplotlib seaborn scikit-learn nltk --break-system-packages

-----------
RUNNING THE CODE
-----------
1. Ensure fake_or_real_news.csv is in the same directory as naive_bayes_classifier.py

2. Run the script:

    python naive_bayes_classifier.py

   (or: python3 naive_bayes_classifier.py)

3. The script will automatically:
   - Download required NLTK resources (stopwords) on first run
   - Train and evaluate 6 pipeline variants
   - Print a metrics summary table to the console
   - Save 6 figures to the figures/ subdirectory
   - Save pipeline_results.csv

Expected runtime: ~30-60 seconds on a modern laptop.

-----------
DATASET
-----------
Source: Lutz Hamel (2021). "Fake or Real News Dataset."
        https://github.com/lutzhamel/fake-news

6,335 labeled news articles (3,164 FAKE, 3,171 REAL).
The CSV must contain columns: id, title, text, label

-----------
OUTPUT FIGURES
-----------
fig1_f1_comparison.png      F1 score horizontal bar chart by pipeline
fig2_confusion_matrix.png   Confusion matrix for best model
fig3_roc_curves.png         ROC curves with AUC for all pipelines
fig4_top_features.png       Top 20 predictive words per class
fig5_metrics_comparison.png Grouped bar chart of all 4 metrics
fig6_cv_f1.png              5-fold CV F1 with error bars
